WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:02.427
(roaring)

00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:06.980
- [Narrator] The newest
Jurassic World installment

00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:10.150
is upon us like a hungry T-Rex,

00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:14.440
who doesn't have any
feathers for some reason.

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:16.460
Here's a few things Hollywood

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:19.251
is still getting wrong about dinosaurs.

00:00:19.251 --> 00:00:22.001
(dramatic music)

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:25.370
Many dinosaurs had feathers.

00:00:25.370 --> 00:00:27.910
But the dinosaurs of Jurassic World

00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:31.860
are covered in scales like
crocodiles and lizards.

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:34.230
They might have made an effort to stick in

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:36.780
a few feathers on this dinosaur,

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:40.170
but even if they did,
that's not a real animal;

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:42.980
it's a hybrid of two different species,

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:45.040
which brings us to our next point.

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:47.133
Genetics doesn't work that way.

00:00:48.536 --> 00:00:50.220
- What is that thing?

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:51.053
- They made it.

00:00:51.053 --> 00:00:52.940
- [Announcer] This is the
most dangerous creature

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:54.984
that ever walked the Earth.

00:00:54.984 --> 00:00:55.817
(growling)

00:00:55.817 --> 00:00:58.340
- [Narrator] Sure, some species can breed.

00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:00.780
That's how you get a liger.

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:03.930
But the Indoraptor isn't like a liger.

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:07.250
It's a mix of a type of
tyrannosaur and raptor,

00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:10.720
two very different kinds of animals.

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:12.720
It would be more like
if you tried to breed

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:15.400
a lion with a wolf instead.

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:17.510
It's just not going to happen.

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:20.100
The DNA isn't compatible.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:22.267
(roaring)

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:26.440
Dinosaurs didn't roar.

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:29.530
In the first Jurassic World, T-Rex's roar

00:01:29.530 --> 00:01:32.060
was made from a mix of animal sounds,

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:34.680
and it sounds pretty
similar in the new film.

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:37.620
But dinosaurs didn't roar like a lion.

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:41.070
They're related to birds,
which means their voice box

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:43.630
was probably similar to a bird's.

00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:45.690
Raptors had wings.

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:49.020
First of all, raptor hands
didn't droop down like that.

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:51.130
In reality, that would only happen

00:01:51.130 --> 00:01:53.050
if their wrists were broken.

00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:57.140
In truth, raptors, like
birds today, had wings.

00:01:57.140 --> 00:02:00.290
They couldn't fly, but the wing shape

00:02:00.290 --> 00:02:02.903
means their arms look nothing like this.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:06.510
Dinosaurs were colorful.

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:10.533
Yeah, Jurassic World's
dinosaurs are way too dull.

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:13.780
Sure, earth tones like green and brown

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:16.400
are common in today's reptiles,

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:18.310
but paleontologists have found

00:02:18.310 --> 00:02:22.140
that dinosaurs came in a
kaleidoscope of bright colors,

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:24.640
just like today's birds.

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:27.793
Some even had shiny, iridescent feathers.

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:30.580
We get it, Hollywood.

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:33.459
T-Rex is scarier with its iconic roar.

00:02:33.459 --> 00:02:35.626
(roaring)

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:39.820
And velociraptors looked more vicious

00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:42.100
without those ridiculous feathers.

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:43.650
But come on!

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:47.045
You could at least add
a little more color!

00:02:47.045 --> 00:02:49.130
(sighs) Maybe next time.

00:02:49.130 --> 00:02:50.350
- It was all a lie!

00:02:50.350 --> 00:02:52.360
- [Narrator] This video
was made in large part

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:55.260
thanks to paleontologist Steve Brusatte,

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:58.173
author of The Rise and
Fall of the Dinosaurs.

